WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.090
Film Courage: Dan what about the writer who
is too loose, too sloppy, doesn’t check

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:06.110
everything out “Oh everything is going to
be fine.”

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.960
Because they are almost like two of the same?

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.160
Daniel Calvisi, movie studio reader, screenwriter
and private screenplay consultant: …Two

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.210
sides of the same coin?

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:14.650
Well, that’s where a consultant like me
can really help you?

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:19.720
[Gives example of a critique] Okay this is
not a professional level quality.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.550
I was confused in places.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.779
I really felt like scenes went on too long.

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:31.660
I felt like these voices weren’t authentic
in dialogue.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.680
So I’m the kind of person, a writing coach,
a story consultant who can really help someone

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.480
like that and guide them to improve their
craft.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:52.739
Film Courage: So both of those traits - perfectionist
versus loose non-detail oriented, where does

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:53.739
that stem from?

00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:54.770
Dan: Where you fall on the spectrum?

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.690
Film Courage: No…I’m wondering how does
someone become a perfectionist?

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.440
How does someone become sort of loose kinda
of…

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:02.840
Dan: Kind of a lazy writer?

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:06.950
Film Courage: I’m not sure if it’s so
much lazy but they don’t seem to care as

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:10.670
much about details because they think they
don’t matter.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:14.619
Maybe they are willing to spend time on stuff
that does matter so they are not lazy in that

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:15.619
respect.

00:01:15.619 --> 00:01:18.939
But in terms of tying up loose ends “Ah…they’ll
understand.”

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:22.819
There are just some people that flow with
some things in some ways and are more controlled

00:01:22.819 --> 00:01:24.109
in other ways.

00:01:24.109 --> 00:01:26.270
Where does that come from?

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:29.079
Dan: Well I think it comes from your personality.

00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:35.749
I think it comes from your own personal experience,
how you approach creative work.

00:01:35.749 --> 00:01:42.420
But it is a craft and it’s going to take
a long time to learn that craft and really

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.840
perfect it and to write your best work, to
find your voice and write the best thing that

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:53.569
you were born to write and it’s going to
take time and if you are really loose and

00:01:53.569 --> 00:01:57.189
what you might call lazy just you’re not
detail-oriented, you’re not specific, you

00:01:57.189 --> 00:02:02.489
just don’t care if the scene is confusing
because it makes sense to you.

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:05.320
Well that’s going to be a problem because
the reader cares.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:10.509
Because the reader wants it to makes sense
and wants it to be tight and wants it to have

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:16.140
a really great arc and surprise and subvert
their expectations and wants it to just be

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:17.430
great.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:24.680
So if you are a really loose writer then maybe
screenwriting is not quite the discipline

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:25.680
for you.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:26.709
Film Courage: Sure.

00:02:26.709 --> 00:02:33.090
Then the flip side of one that can’t let
it go because there are just too many variables

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:38.680
that they’ve got to make sure are right
but then the life is squeezed out because

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:39.680
it’s so tight.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:45.980
Dan: It can become dryer the more you pick
at it and pick at it.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:51.590
I can’t say specifically which draft does
that happen.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:57.170
Okay, between draft 12 and 15 it’s going
to get way too dry and you’re going to lose

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:59.580
that original essence.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.709
But that is that instance in which someone
picks at something and picks at something,

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:10.950
rewrites it so many times, takes every single
note they get from every friend and every

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:17.260
consultant and it ends up just kind of bleeding
it dry and you lose the essence of the original

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:22.950
idea, the original concept what made you passionate
about that.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.940
That’s a pitfall too to look out for.

